#
# Tepna — fold the box nights the corpus does not hold yet (E12,
# briefs/NIGHT-0928-ERRORS-2026-09-29-BRIEF.md). Measured 2026-09-29: nothing folded a box night
# automatically, the newest folded night was 2026-09-21, and the backlog was SEVEN nights —
# `tepna-nightly-triage` runs only the mutation crawl. A backlog is not a neutral state: a night that is
# not folded is a night whose QC, hat and trio numbers nobody can read.
#
# 🔴 NO NEW TOOL. `tools/trio-batch.mjs --skip-existing` IS the "fold only what is due" decider — its
# `redoReason` is a pure function returning why a night must be recomputed (no stamp, `inputs changed`,
# `code changed`) or null when skipping is provably safe, and the tool already emits ONE
# tepna.verdict/1. A second decider would be a second definition of "due", free to drift from the one
# the fold actually uses. This unit is therefore a SCHEDULE and nothing else.
#
# ⚠️ NOTHING IS PUSHED AND NOTHING IS COMMITTED, and that is structural rather than a promise: every
# path `trio-batch` writes is under its `--out` root, and the repo ignores `uploads/*`, so a fold
# produces no file git can see. There is no local branch to make and no push to withhold — which is a
# stronger guarantee than "the timer must not push", because it does not depend on the timer behaving.
# N-of-1 results stay off GitHub for the same reason the tchref ledger does.
#
# 🔴 NO REDIRECT, AND THE VERDICT IS NOT THIS UNIT'S TO WRITE. `trio-batch` already persists it as
# `$FOLD_OUT/trio-batch-verdicts.json` ({ run, nights }) beside the fold outputs. My first draft of this
# unit piped stdout into a `FOLD_VERDICT` file, which would have been wrong twice: `--json` adds ONE
# verdict line to a stdout that also carries the run's prose, so the "`.json`" file would not have been
# JSON — and it would have been a SECOND verdict artefact competing with the tool's own. Stdout belongs
# in the journal; the verdict belongs where the tool puts it.
#
# The corpus paths come from an EnvironmentFile the owner writes: nothing in this file names a corpus
# directory, a host, or a home path.
#
# INSTALL — the corpus machine only, and NOT performed by the PR that adds this file:
#   1. give the timer its OWN detached worktree at ~/wt-fold-timer (nobody edits there), and symlink
#      ~/Tepna/node_modules into it;
#   2. mkdir -p ~/.config/tepna and write fold.env with FOLD_SRC (the captures root) and FOLD_OUT (the
#      corpus trio root) — two variables, both directories, neither of them a verdict path;
#   3. copy this pair into ~/.config/systemd/user/, then daemon-reload and enable the .timer.
# A separate checkout from the release and tchref timers' means no two timers race one detach, and
# because nobody edits there, fast-forwarding it before each tick is safe.
[Unit]
Description=Tepna — fold the box nights the corpus does not hold yet
Documentation=https://github.com/Plantucha/Tepna/blob/main/tools/trio-batch.mjs

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=%h/wt-fold-timer
EnvironmentFile=%h/.config/tepna/fold.env
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/git fetch -q origin main
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/git switch -q --detach origin/main
# A missing tool is "not on main yet", exit 0 — NOT a green verdict: nothing is written, so the previous
# verdict file keeps its own `at` stamp and reads as stale rather than as a pass. An incomplete env is
# exit 2, a refusal, because a fold pointed at the wrong root writes a corpus where nobody looks.
# ⚠️ NEVER add `--jobs`: forcing the fold's parallelism is a standing fleet rule. The tool sizes itself
# against the box; a fixed count either starves peers' gates or overruns memory.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c '[ -f tools/trio-batch.mjs ] || { echo "trio-batch.mjs not on origin/main yet — nothing to run"; exit 0; }; [ -n "$FOLD_SRC" ] && [ -n "$FOLD_OUT" ] || { echo "fold.env incomplete (FOLD_SRC / FOLD_OUT)"; exit 2; }; [ -d "$FOLD_SRC" ] || { echo "FOLD_SRC is not a directory: $FOLD_SRC"; exit 2; }; exec node tools/trio-batch.mjs --src "$FOLD_SRC" --out "$FOLD_OUT" --skip-existing --json'
